# RoomSpace — Weekly Developer Journal

These entries summarize the work recorded in the project journal and repository. Each week captures the goal, the approach and outcome, the main difficulty or decision, and the next step.

## Week of 24–30 August 2026

**Goal:** Define the RoomSpace problem and agree on a workable project scope.

**What I worked on:** Finalized the project proposal for a constraint-based room planning and decoration recommendation system. It describes the problem, workflow, web architecture, spatial constraints, evaluation, scalability, risks, and deliverables.

**What was unclear / what I tried:** The initial scope covered room analysis, layout generation, visualization, commands, versioning, and recommendations. Treating all of these as equally urgent made the first implementation steps hard to identify.

**What worked / result:** We split the system into modules and prioritized the core room-model and layout workflow before advanced ML and recommendation work.

**Takeaway / next step:** A broad idea becomes easier to build once the first end-to-end workflow is explicit. Next, establish the repository structure and validated room data model.

## Week of 31 August–6 September 2026

**Goal:** Turn the proposal into an initial architecture and backend foundation.

**What I worked on:** Organized the repository into frontend, backend, ML, documentation, and supporting areas. Started the FastAPI backend and defined validated Room schemas for dimensions, room type, theme, occasion, and budget. Added room creation with UUIDs, a health check, and Swagger documentation.

**What was unclear / what I tried:** Persistence and spatial planning depend on a consistent room representation. I established and validated that representation first, so later services can build on a stable API contract.

**What worked / result:** The backend can accept a valid room request, create a uniquely identified room, and expose endpoints for inspection.

**Takeaway / next step:** Define data contracts before layering on planning logic. Next, make the planned user flow visible and investigate product data.

## Week of 7–13 September 2026

**Goal:** Make the project flow demonstrable and start connecting room understanding with recommendations.

**What I worked on:** Added the initial Vite, React, TypeScript, and Tailwind frontend. Implemented room analysis and furniture detection using pretrained YOLOv8, added layout and placement schemas and room-management/validation features, and started zero-shot product-to-style matching with pretrained CLIP. The frontend uses local fallback data where backend integration is not yet reliable.

**What was unclear / what I tried:** Frontend integration and product-data preparation were not ready to support every part of the demo. I used local fallback data to keep the user flow moving, while identifying useful product attributes such as category, style, dimensions, and price.

**What worked / result:** The initial frontend and backend capabilities can be demonstrated while the product data pipeline is still being prepared. Pretrained models provide a starting point without requiring custom training immediately.

**Takeaway / next step:** Keep interfaces usable while integrations mature, and defer fine-tuning until the core workflow shows where it is needed. Next, improve analysis and connect detected objects to practical room placements.

## Week of 14–20 September 2026

**Goal:** Consolidate the initial architecture and decide how ML work fits the room-planning workflow.

**What I worked on:** Finalized the project workflow and backend foundation, and restructured the frontend by module while adding a dark-theme redesign. Continued room-analysis and layout-detection work. The chosen approach is pretrained YOLO for room-object detection, with fine-tuning only if needed, and CLIP for product recommendation.

**What was unclear / what I tried:** Starting custom training before the room-planning flow was stable risked spending effort on a model before its role was clear. I prioritized the core room model, layout engine, constraints, and visualization first.

**What worked / result:** The project has a clearer implementation order: build and validate the room-planning path, then evaluate whether model customization is necessary.

**Takeaway / next step:** ML choices should support a defined product workflow. Next, improve room analysis and translate detected furniture into usable placements.

## Week of 5–11 October 2026

**Goal:** Make detected furniture useful for planning by estimating its size in the room.

**What I worked on:** Improved room analysis to estimate furniture dimensions relative to the known room size and added a local YOLOv8 model for detecting objects in room images.

**What was unclear / what I tried:** Detection identifies objects but does not provide enough information to plan around them. Using the known room size gives the analysis a reference for estimating furniture dimensions.

**What worked / result:** The analysis now provides object detections and estimated dimensions that can feed later layout work.

**Takeaway / next step:** Detection needs room-scale context to support placement. Next, connect the analysis output to a generated room layout.

## Week of 12–18 October 2026

**Goal:** Turn room-analysis output into a generated layout.

**What I worked on:** Added an endpoint for generating a room layout from detected furniture.

**What was unclear / what I tried:** Object detection on its own did not produce a usable plan. I connected the detection results to the layout-generation workflow through the new endpoint.

**What worked / result:** The backend now has a path from detected furniture to a layout-generation request.

**Takeaway / next step:** Keep the boundary between analysis and layout explicit. Next, map detected furniture to structured placements and room zones.

## Week of 19–25 October 2026

**Goal:** Represent furniture placements in a way the layout workflow can use.

**What I worked on:** Introduced a zone-placement service that maps detected furniture to placements within room zones.

**What was unclear / what I tried:** Raw detections needed to become structured placements before they could be used by layout generation. The placement service provides that translation.

**What worked / result:** Detected furniture can now be represented as placements associated with room zones.

**Takeaway / next step:** Separating detection from placement keeps the workflow easier to reason about. Next, make the backend flow easier to explore in a demonstration.

## Week of 26 October–1 November 2026

**Goal:** Provide a simple way to demonstrate and inspect the room-planning workflow.

**What I worked on:** Added a live-demo HTML page for interacting with the backend. Continued room-analysis and layout-detection improvements recorded in the repository.

**What was unclear / what I tried:** Backend capabilities needed a straightforward entry point for trying the workflow. I added a lightweight demo page so users can interact with the service without first building a separate demo client.

**What worked / result:** The project has a browser-based way to explore the backend workflow, alongside the ongoing analysis and layout work.

**Takeaway / next step:** A demo makes integration gaps easier to see. Next, exercise the end-to-end path, document remaining limitations, and continue improving layout quality.
